sycl: indexed attention bias in the flash-attention tile kernel

HomeBrain patch on ggml-sycl. Applies to the pin directly.
Upstream tracking: https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/issues/32757

llama.cpp hands Inkling 2's attention a compact bias (#32740 and the Inkling
model): a table [n_bins, n_heads, n_queries] F32 and a bin per key and query.
ggml-sycl rejected the node, so llama replaced the bias with a dense
[keys x queries x heads] mask, built every pass. The tile kernel's
ncols2 == 1 variants (head size 64/128) now add table[bin] to the mask in
F32, as the CPU does; a bin outside the table adds nothing. Sinks move to
the final scale or the combine pass, as in CUDA. oneDNN, MKL, DPAS, VEC and
the sparse gather never see a node that carries the bias (0015 and 0018 add
it to MKL and DPAS). test-backend-ops FLASH_ATTN_EXT -p n_bins passes against
the CPU; the kv_rows cases are not supported on SYCL and fall back.

